WEBVTT

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:04.440
Threat modeling helps teams ask how a feature can be abused before the implementation

00:00:04.660 --> 00:00:06.660
hardens into production behavior.

00:00:06.880 --> 00:00:08.320
Without that step,

00:00:08.540 --> 00:00:13.180
teams may protect individual endpoints while leaving unsafe business flows intact.

00:00:13.400 --> 00:00:17.320
Security requirements translate risk into design constraints.

00:00:17.540 --> 00:00:23.380
Requirements such as rate limits, approval gates, auditability, tenant isolation,

00:00:23.600 --> 00:00:26.800
and fraud controls should be visible before coding begins.

00:00:27.020 --> 00:00:31.660
Unsafe architectural assumptions often appear when services trust internal networks,

00:00:31.880 --> 00:00:36.600
client-side state, partner integrations, or sequence order more than they should.

00:00:36.820 --> 00:00:41.060
Attackers routinely exploit those assumptions rather than a single defective line of code.

00:00:41.280 --> 00:00:43.920
Insecure design is not fixed by scanning alone.

00:00:44.140 --> 00:00:49.340
It requires review of workflows, trust boundaries, misuse cases, control ownership,

00:00:49.560 --> 00:00:53.000
and the decisions that determine how the application is supposed to behave.

00:00:53.220 --> 00:00:56.980
Use a secure SDLC with security requirements at design time.

00:00:57.200 --> 00:01:00.400
Run threat modeling for critical workflows and trust boundaries.

00:01:00.620 --> 00:01:03.980
Perform security architecture reviews before major releases.

00:01:04.200 --> 00:01:09.160
Selected NIST mappings for this topic include SA-3 - System Development Life Cycle;

00:01:09.380 --> 00:01:14.980
SA-8 - Security and Privacy Engineering Principles; RA-3 - Risk Assessment.
